# Macro Trends {#sec-macro}

```{r}
#| label: fig-timeseries
#| fig-cap: "Time series — percentage of congressional tweets about the deficit (by party), June 2017–Jan 2023. Shaded bands mark legislative windows."
knitr::include_graphics("/Users/cycoldiron/Desktop/congress-fiscal-tweets/figures/summary/deficit_tweet_share_with_legislation.png")
```

@fig-timeseries shows the monthly share of congressional deficit tweets, by party, from June 2017 to January 2023. Consistent with political science best practices, this paper will represent the Republican party with red shading, and the Democratic party with blue. Shaded areas indicate legislative periods – defined as the month before, during and after the passage of a fiscally significant bill, which excludes small appropriations, procedural votes, and commemorations.

Baseline deficit tweeting share for both parties is low – generally staying under 1% across the six-year period with notable accelerations occur around legislative periods. The minority party—Democrats from 2017 to 2021 and Republicans from 2021 to 2023—typically tweets at higher rates than the majority party, as illustrated in the peaks for Democrats (2017) and Republicans (2022, 2023).

